Wait out a predecessor that is still dying.

A pusher that finds the writer lease held retries until the store lets
it in, and patch 0023 gave it the right bound: wait as far out as the
expiry the store reports, and stop early if that expiry is seen to move,
because a lease being renewed is a node that is alive and this cluster
has been replaced.

Seen to move once is not proof. A node killed hard does not stop the
instant it is killed. Its renewal thread lives in a process that runs
until whatever store call it is inside of returns, so a cluster that is
already dead can push its expiry forward two or three more times while
the node taking over is asking for the lease. That is what the soak
caught at iteration 5: the whole node killed at 08:24:05, the fresh
attach asking from 08:24:21, the dead node's expiry moving at 08:24:19
and again at 08:24:24, and the successor calling that a live rival and
shutting itself down four seconds into a takeover nobody was contending.
The lease holder in the refusal was the dead node's own data directory,
which is how it was pinned down.

What a dying holder cannot do is keep it up. Its renewals stop when its
process does, which is seconds, while a node that is really running
renews for as long as it runs. So the exit now wants the expiry to keep
advancing for thirty seconds, two busy TTLs, rather than to advance
once. A thaw still stops, half a minute later than before and long
before a client has given up on it, and a failover no longer stops at
all.

The other half of this fix is in the crate, where the renewal thread now
stops the first time it wakes up in a process the postmaster has left
behind. This side stands on its own: it is the successor's judgement,
and it should not depend on every predecessor being well behaved.

diff --git a/src/backend/access/transam/zou_wal.c b/src/backend/access/transam/zou_wal.c
index 9199c7d..979282d 100644
--- a/src/backend/access/transam/zou_wal.c
+++ b/src/backend/access/transam/zou_wal.c
@@ -360,6 +360,22 @@ zou_lease_wait_secs(void)
  */
 #define ZOU_LEASE_EXPIRY_GRACE 5
 
+/*
+ * How long the expiry has to keep moving before a waiter calls the
+ * holder alive rather than dying.
+ *
+ * One move is not enough. A node killed hard leaves its renewal thread
+ * running for as long as the store call it was inside of takes to
+ * return, so the expiry of a cluster that is already gone can advance a
+ * few more times while its successor is asking, and treating the first
+ * of those as proof of a live node makes the successor stop on the very
+ * failover it exists to perform. What a dying holder cannot do is keep
+ * it up: its renewals stop when its process does. Thirty seconds is two
+ * busy TTLs, longer than any wind down and short enough that a cluster
+ * that really has been replaced stops before a client gives up on it.
+ */
+#define ZOU_LEASE_MOVING_SECS 30
+
 /*
  * How long a stopping pusher spends getting the store a checkpoint worth
  * starting from, before it gives up and goes anyway.
@@ -399,6 +415,7 @@ ZouWalPusherMain(Datum main_arg)
 	int			fold_backoff = 0;
 	int			waited;
 	uint64		expiry_seen = 0;
+	pg_time_t	moving_since = 0;
 	uint64		resume = 0;
 	char	   *buf;
 
@@ -447,13 +464,21 @@ ZouWalPusherMain(Datum main_arg)
